A branching strategy is a workflow that defines how developers create, manage, merge, and release code changes in Git. 
It helps teams collaborate without affecting main code.

In my project, we used Git Flow branching strategy.
We had separate branches for development, testing, and production releases.

Branches Used
main/master → Production code
develop → latest development changes
feature/* → New feature development to work
release/* → Release preparation and testing
hotfix/* → Production bug fixes after deployment 

We had multiple developers working simultaneously and separate environments such as Dev, QA, UAT, and Production.
Git Flow helped us isolate features, perform testing properly, and manage releases without affecting production.

In my project, we used Git Flow. Developers created feature branches from the develop branch. 
Once development was completed, they raised Pull Requests for code review. After approval, the code was merged into develop, 
which triggered our Azure DevOps/Jenkins CI pipeline for build, testing, SonarQube analysis, and artifact creation. 
For releases, we created release branches and deployed them to QA and UAT environments. 
After successful testing, the release branch was merged into the main branch, 
which triggered the production deployment pipeline. For urgent production issues, we used hotfix branches directly from main.

